Oracle Database Backup & Recovery — Notes
1. What is Backup?

A backup is a copy of database information that can be used to restore the database after a failure.

DATABASE
    ↓
  BACKUP
    ↓
Backup copy
    ↓
Failure
    ↓
RESTORE
    ↓
RECOVERY
Why do we need backup?

Backup protects against:

Datafile loss/corruption
Disk failure
Database failure
Accidental deletion
Hardware failure
Disaster
User mistakes
2. Backup vs Restore vs Recovery

These three terms are very important.

BACKUP

Create a copy.

Database → Backup
RESTORE

Bring the backup copy back to the required location.

Backup → Datafile
RECOVER

Apply redo/archived redo to bring the restored files up to the required point.

Restored Datafile
       +
Redo / Archived Redo
       ↓
   RECOVERY
Easy way to remember

Backup = Copy
Restore = Put the copy back
Recover = Apply changes after the backup

3. Physical vs Logical Backup

There are two broad types.

                 BACKUP
                    │
          ┌─────────┴─────────┐
          ↓                   ↓
      PHYSICAL             LOGICAL
Physical Backup

Copies physical database files.

Examples:

Datafiles
Control files
SPFILE
Archived redo logs

The main Oracle tool is:

RMAN — Recovery Manager

Logical Backup

Exports database objects such as:

Tables
Schemas
Users' objects
Metadata

Oracle provides Data Pump Export (expdp) for this.

4. Cold Backup

A cold backup is taken when the database is shut down.

Database OPEN
      ↓
SHUTDOWN
      ↓
Database CLOSED
      ↓
Copy database files
      ↓
Backup

Because the database is not running, the files are not changing during the copy.

Also called:

Offline backup

5. Hot Backup

A hot backup is taken while the database is open.

DATABASE
   ↓
OPEN
   ↓
Users working
   ↓
BACKUP

Also called:

Online backup

For production databases, online backups are very useful because you don't have to stop the database.

RMAN supports online database backup when the database is configured appropriately, typically with ARCHIVELOG mode.

6. NOARCHIVELOG Mode

In NOARCHIVELOG mode:

Redo
 ↓
Online Redo Logs
 ↓
Not archived

Recovery capability is limited.

Suppose:

10:00 → Backup
11:00 → Transactions
12:00 → Datafile failure

In NOARCHIVELOG mode:

10:00 Backup
      ↓
Restore
      ↓
Changes after backup
      ↓
May be LOST

Therefore:

NOARCHIVELOG is suitable for simple development/test environments where some data loss can be accepted.

7. ARCHIVELOG Mode

In ARCHIVELOG mode, Oracle preserves redo information by archiving filled online redo logs.

Online Redo Log
       ↓
   Log Switch
       ↓
Archived Redo Log

The archived redo can be used during recovery.

Example:

10:00 → Backup
10:30 → Transactions
11:00 → Transactions
11:30 → Datafile failure

Recovery can be:

Backup
   ↓
Restore
   ↓
Apply archived redo
   ↓
Recover database

This allows recovery much closer to the failure point, depending on the available backups and redo.

8. Online Redo Log vs Archived Redo Log
Online Redo Log

Used continuously by the running database.

Transaction
    ↓
Redo generated
    ↓
Online Redo Log
Archived Redo Log

Preserved redo information from an online redo log before it is reused.

Online Redo Log
       ↓
    FULL
       ↓
  Log Switch
       ↓
Archived Redo Log
Remember

Online redo = current/reusable redo

Archived redo = preserved redo used for recovery

9. FRA — Fast Recovery Area

FRA stands for:

Fast Recovery Area

It is an Oracle-managed storage location for recovery-related files.

For example:

D:\LABS\FRA

It can contain:

FRA
 │
 ├── RMAN Backups
 ├── Archived Redo Logs
 └── Flashback Logs

Configure it with:

ALTER SYSTEM SET db_recovery_file_dest='D:\LABS\FRA'
SCOPE=BOTH;

Set its size:

ALTER SYSTEM SET db_recovery_file_dest_size=8G
SCOPE=BOTH;

Check:

SHOW PARAMETER db_recovery;
10. RMAN

RMAN = Recovery Manager

It is Oracle's primary tool for physical backup and recovery.

Start RMAN:

rman target /

You will see:

RMAN>

Now you can execute RMAN commands.

11. Full Database Backup

Basic command:

BACKUP DATABASE;

This backs up the database's datafiles.

A common practical backup is:

BACKUP DATABASE PLUS ARCHIVELOG;

This backs up:

Database
   +
Archived Redo Logs

For an ARCHIVELOG recovery strategy, having both is important.

12. Backup a Specific Datafile

Suppose datafile 7 is your USERS datafile.

You can use:

BACKUP DATAFILE 7;

Or by file name:

BACKUP DATAFILE 'D:\LABS\USERS01.DBF';
13. Backup Control File

The control file is extremely important because it contains database structural information.

RMAN:

BACKUP CURRENT CONTROLFILE;

You can also configure automatic control-file/SPFILE backups:

CONFIGURE CONTROLFILE AUTOBACKUP ON;

Check configuration:

SHOW ALL;
14. Backup SPFILE

You can back up the SPFILE:

BACKUP SPFILE;

Remember:

PFILE → Text
SPFILE → Binary
15. Backup Archived Redo Logs
BACKUP ARCHIVELOG ALL;

This preserves archived redo for recovery.

16. List Backups

Inside RMAN:

LIST BACKUP;

This shows information about RMAN backups.

You can also:

LIST BACKUP OF DATABASE;
17. Check Database Backup

RMAN can validate backups:

VALIDATE DATABASE;

This checks whether database files can be read and are suitable for backup/recovery purposes.

18. Restore

Suppose a datafile is lost.

Conceptually:

Datafile lost
     ↓
RESTORE
     ↓
Backup copy brought back

RMAN:

RESTORE DATAFILE 7;

But restore alone is not necessarily enough.

You may then need:

RECOVER DATAFILE 7;
19. Restore vs Recover

This is an important interview/exam question.

RESTORE

Gets the file from backup.

Backup
  ↓
RESTORE
  ↓
Datafile
RECOVER

Applies redo to the restored file.

Datafile
   +
Archived Redo
   ↓
RECOVER
   ↓
Current/required state

Therefore:

RESTORE gets the old copy back; RECOVER brings it forward using redo.

20. Complete Media Recovery

Suppose:

USERS01.DBF

is lost.

The general process is:

1. Identify failed datafile
          ↓
2. Restore datafile
          ↓
3. Recover datafile
          ↓
4. Bring it online

Example:

RESTORE DATAFILE 7;
RECOVER DATAFILE 7;

Then, depending on its state:

ALTER DATABASE DATAFILE 7 ONLINE;
21. Instance Recovery

Instance failure is different.

Example:

Server crashes
       ↓
Oracle instance stops
       ↓
Database files are still present
       ↓
STARTUP
       ↓
Oracle performs instance recovery

Oracle uses:

Online redo
Undo
Checkpoint information

to make the database consistent.

You normally do not restore a backup just because of a normal instance crash.

22. Media Recovery

Media failure means physical files are lost or damaged.

Example:

Disk failure
     ↓
USERS01.DBF lost
     ↓
Restore backup
     ↓
Apply redo
     ↓
Recovery

This is where ARCHIVELOG becomes especially important.

23. Checkpoint and Recovery

You previously looked at:

SELECT file#, checkpoint_change#
FROM v$datafile;

For example:

FILE#   CHECKPOINT_CHANGE#
1       605006
2       605006
3       605006
4       605006

605006 is the checkpoint SCN.

If datafiles have the same checkpoint SCN, they are synchronized with respect to that checkpoint.

SCN

SCN = System Change Number

Oracle uses SCNs to track database changes and recovery progress.

24. Why Checkpoints Matter

Think:

Database changes
      ↓
Redo generated
      ↓
Checkpoint
      ↓
Dirty blocks written to datafiles
      ↓
Checkpoint SCN recorded

During recovery, Oracle uses checkpoint information to determine where recovery needs to begin.

25. Backup Strategy

A production backup strategy should consider:

Database
   │
   ├── Datafile backups
   ├── Controlfile backup
   ├── SPFILE backup
   └── Archived redo backups

And importantly:

Don't keep your only backup on the same physical disk as the database.

For example, if:

Database → D:
Backup   → D:

and the D: disk fails, you may lose both.

Better:

Database → D:
Backup   → E: / Network / External storage
